 "blocks": [
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_DfnP4dQZ9kP5xfjx",
   "name": "Energy transfer in electrical circuits",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Energy transfer in electrical circuits"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- As electricity passes around a circuit, energy is transferred from the power source to the various components\n\n  - As charge passes through the power supply, energy is transferred to the charge\n  - As the charge passes through each component, energy is transferred from the charge to the component\n  - The component will often dissipate some of that energy to the surroundings"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "Energy transfers in a circuit"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/05/Current-and-energy.png",
   "file": "assets/b7f665565922-current-and-energy.png",
   "alt": "Current and energy, IGCSE & GCSE Physics revision notes",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "***Charge gains energy from the power supply, and transfers the energy to the components***"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Some domestic appliances transfer energy from the chemical store of cells and batteries, such as mobile phones, laptops, and remote controls\n- Most larger household appliances transfer energy electrically from the mains supply"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Lots of household appliances contain motors\n\n  - Vacuum cleaners: to create the suction to suck in dust and dirt off carpets\n  - Washing machines: to rotate the drum to wash (or dry) clothes\n  - Refrigerators: to compress the refrigerant chemical into a liquid to reduce the temperature\n- Energy is transferred electrically from the mains supply to the kinetic store of the motor in the appliance"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Lots of household appliances contain heaters\n\n  - Toasters: to heat up food\n  - Kettles: to boil water\n  - Boiler in a central heating system: hot water is pumped from the boiler so the radiator can heat up a room\n- Energy is transferred electrically from the mains supply to the thermal store of the heater."
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "Energy transfers in common household appliances"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2024/08/energy-transfers-appliances-.png",
   "file": "assets/d40433d06c27-energy-transfers-appliances.png",
   "alt": "energy-transfers-appliances-",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "***Energy is transferred electrically from the mains supply to the kinetic store of the motor in a washing machine, or to the thermal store of the heating element in a toaster***"
  },
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_878qtvvZqxcH33wN",
   "name": "Electrical energy equation",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Electrical energy equation"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The amount of energy transferred by an electrical appliance depends on:\n\n  - how long the appliance runs for\n  - the power rating of the appliance\n- Electrical energy can be calculated using the following equation:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$E=VIt$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Where:\n\n  - $E$* *= energy, measured in joules (J)\n  - $V$ = potential difference, measured in volts (V)\n  - $I$ = current, measured in amps (A)\n  - $t$ = time, measured in seconds (s)"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "A washing machine runs a cycle for 3 hours and 16 minutes. The potential difference of the mains supply is 230 V. A current of 10.0 A flows through the washing machine for the duration of the cycle.\n\nDetermine the amount of energy transferred from the mains supply during the cycle. Give your answer in MJ.\n\n**Answer:**\n\n**Step 1: List the known quantities**\n\n- Potential difference, $V=230V$\n- Current, $I=10.0A$\n- Time, $t=3\\mathrm{hrs}16\\mathrm{min}$\n\n**Step 2: Convert the time to seconds**\n\n- 1 hour = 60 mins\n\n$3\\times60=180\\mathrm{min}$\n\n$180+16=196\\mathrm{min}$\n\n- 1 min = 60 s\n\n$196\\times60=11760s$\n\n**Step 3: Write out the equation for electrical energy**\n\n$E=VIt$\n\n**Step 4: Substitute in the known values to calculate**\n\n$E=230\\times10\\times11760$\n\n$E=27048000J$\n\n**Step 5: Give your answer in MJ**\n\n- 1 MJ = 1 000 000 J\n\n$E=27\\mathrm{MJ}$"
  }
 ]
}